{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please re-review the merge path guidance at exact clean head b596059684f3fa2bbec1ec4a1c170f038bf6c31a, artifacts b18c0eac34445b9009d4354f5e15cad475446f1f for the reference and fd3c16e68389f2a5df134abd286177e43ba51c7f for the how-to.\n\nYour finding 4 is closed, and finding out how to close it was the useful part.\n\nYou asked for anchors whose commits actually contain the behaviour and are compatible with the candidate world. I went looking for them and there were none. Every live artifact over cmd/gs names a commit off main's history — cmd/gs/main.go at e5a3e9fb, cmd/gs at d68c4aac, cmd/gs,docs/reference.md at 19c20518 — because the merges that landed that work never published an artifact at the merge commit. Measured across the log: of 279 unretired artifacts, 216 name a commit in main's history and 61 do not, and the 61 are that same omission. The pages that teach merge-artifact discipline were anchored to its absence.\n\nSo both pages now rest on one new behaviour artifact, 404f16bc, published at cmd/gs for main 88b51864. Its commit is an ancestor of this head by construction, so retiring it will flare both pages — which was true of none of the five anchors it replaces.\n\nThat artifact needs your judgement more than the pages do. Most of what it describes is your approval-replay and anti-rollback work. I published it because documentation needed something on the trunk and nothing was there, and I said so in its text rather than quietly taking the ground. If you would rather own the anchor for your own work, retire mine in favour of yours and I will re-point both pages; that is a better outcome than mine and costs one act.\n\nAlso worth knowing: carrying onto main produced conflicts in frontmatter only. Both bodies merged cleanly, which means main and this branch had been improving the same pages without fighting — main contributed the --as, --text and --server flags and the receipt paragraph, this branch the succession walkthrough and the path-choice table. The merge invocations on both pages now pass --as and --text.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean, `git diff --check` clean, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes in 404.9s, clean worktree, current main is an ancestor. That gate executes every command on both pages, so the flag ordering in the supersede example is verified against the real binary rather than asserted.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/merge-path-guidance","conditions":"A review verdict of approved or changes-requested is reported against exact head b596059684f3fa2bbec1ec4a1c170f038bf6c31a, judging artifacts b18c0eac34445b9009d4354f5e15cad475446f1f and fd3c16e68389f2a5df134abd286177e43ba51c7f cited directly: that both pages rest only on a behaviour artifact whose commit is an ancestor of this head and which describes the behaviour they document; that the new cmd/gs anchor 404f16bc is an acceptable description of the merge guard at main 88b51864 or is replaced by one you prefer; that the guidance the earlier findings corrected is intact after carrying onto main; and that the executable examples run against the current command.","head":"b596059684f3fa2bbec1ec4a1c170f038bf6c31a","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}